                      GUIDE: DISKS, FILESYSTEMS AND SWAP
      (arrows/PgUp/PgDn to scroll, / to search, q to quit; "help" lists
                             all commands)

1. LAYOUT
---------
Find the disk with @[lsblk -f]. A simple, good layout for a whole disk:

  UEFI machine (GPT):              BIOS machine (MBR/dos):
    1. EFI system  512 MiB FAT32     1. root   rest of the disk
    2. root        rest of the disk
    (+ swap partition, optional)     (+ swap partition, optional)

Check how this machine booted with @[ls /sys/firmware/efi] (exists: UEFI).
A separate /home makes reinstalling easier; a separate /boot is not needed.

The kernel used here has no initramfs, so it mounts the root filesystem
itself. That works for ext4, XFS and btrfs, but NOT for a root filesystem on
LVM, LUKS (encrypted) or software RAID (mdadm): those need an initramfs.
Other filesystems (/home, data) can be on them; set that up after the install.


2. WHICH FILESYSTEM
-------------------
ext4    The safe default: mature, fast, can be checked and repaired, can grow
        and shrink (shrinking only unmounted). Pick this if unsure.
          @[mkfs.ext4 -L smgl /dev/sdX2]

XFS     Fast with large files and many parallel writes (databases, media,
        servers); can grow but never shrink.
          @[mkfs.xfs -L smgl /dev/sdX2]

btrfs   Copy-on-write: snapshots (roll back after an update gone wrong),
        transparent compression (saves space; compiling a source distro writes
        a lot), checksums of all data, subvolumes instead of partitions. More
        to learn, and it needs the layout below to be used properly.
          @[mkfs.btrfs -L smgl /dev/sdX2]

Whatever you pick for root, also install its tools on the new system: in the
spell-caches step add xfsprogs or btrfs-progs (e2fsprogs is always there).
EFI system partition: always FAT32, @[mkfs.vfat -F 32 /dev/sdX1].


3. BTRFS, PROPERLY
------------------
Put the system in subvolumes rather than the top of the filesystem, so it can
be snapshotted and rolled back without touching /home. A common layout:

  @            /                 @log        /var/log
  @home        /home             @snapshots  /.snapshots

Create them (once, on the freshly formatted filesystem):
  @[mount /dev/sdX2 $ENCHANT_TARGET]
  @[btrfs subvolume create $ENCHANT_TARGET/@]
  @[btrfs subvolume create $ENCHANT_TARGET/@home]
  @[btrfs subvolume create $ENCHANT_TARGET/@log]
  @[btrfs subvolume create $ENCHANT_TARGET/@snapshots]
  @[umount $ENCHANT_TARGET]

Mount them for the install (compress=zstd compresses new data, noatime avoids
a write on every read; $o just saves typing):
  @[o=compress=zstd,noatime]
  @[mount -o subvol=@,$o /dev/sdX2 $ENCHANT_TARGET]
  @[mkdir -p $ENCHANT_TARGET/{home,var/log,.snapshots}]
  @[mount -o subvol=@home,$o /dev/sdX2 $ENCHANT_TARGET/home]
  @[mount -o subvol=@log,$o /dev/sdX2 $ENCHANT_TARGET/var/log]
  @[mount -o subvol=@snapshots,$o /dev/sdX2 $ENCHANT_TARGET/.snapshots]
  (UEFI: then mount the EFI system partition at $ENCHANT_TARGET/boot/efi)

fstab lines for the new system (edit-confs step; btrfs needs no fsck, so the
last field is 0):
  /dev/sdX2  /            btrfs  subvol=@,compress=zstd,noatime          0 0
  /dev/sdX2  /home        btrfs  subvol=@home,compress=zstd,noatime      0 0
  /dev/sdX2  /var/log     btrfs  subvol=@log,compress=zstd,noatime       0 0
  /dev/sdX2  /.snapshots  btrfs  subvol=@snapshots,compress=zstd,noatime 0 0

The kernel has to be told the root subvolume: grub-mkconfig adds
rootflags=subvol=@ by itself; check with
  @[grep rootflags /boot/grub/grub.cfg]   (inside the chroot)

Later, a snapshot of the system before a big update:
  @[btrfs subvolume snapshot -r / /.snapshots/before-update]


4. SWAP
-------
Swap is disk space the kernel uses when memory runs out. Without it, running
out of memory kills programs. A source distro compiles big programs (gcc,
llvm, rust, browsers), which can need several GiB of memory per make job.

  RAM            swap
  up to 4 GiB    2 x RAM
  4-16 GiB       = RAM (or at least 4 GiB)
  over 16 GiB    4-8 GiB, or none
  hibernation    at least the size of RAM

A swap partition (@[mkswap /dev/sdX3], @[swapon /dev/sdX3]; fstab:
"/dev/sdX3 none swap sw 0 0") or a swap file, which is easier to resize:

ext4 / XFS (here 4 GiB; inside the chroot, i.e. on the new system):
  @[dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=4096]
  @[chmod 600 /swapfile && mkswap /swapfile]
  fstab: "/swapfile none swap sw 0 0"

btrfs: a swap file can't be copy-on-write, compressed or in a snapshotted
subvolume, so give it its own subvolume and let btrfs create the file (which
turns copy-on-write and compression off for it):
  @[btrfs subvolume create $ENCHANT_TARGET/@swap]  (with the others)
  mount it like the others at $ENCHANT_TARGET/swap (subvol=@swap), then
  @[btrfs filesystem mkswapfile --size 4g $ENCHANT_TARGET/swap/swapfile]
  fstab: "/dev/sdX2 /swap btrfs subvol=@swap,noatime 0 0" and
         "/swap/swapfile none swap sw 0 0"

Hibernation also needs resume=<swap partition, e.g. PARTUUID=...> on the
kernel command line (GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT in /etc/default/grub).


5. ZRAM: COMPRESSED SWAP IN RAM
-------------------------------
zram is a swap device kept in RAM, compressed (zstd typically fits 2-3 pages
in the space of one). When memory runs short the kernel swaps there first,
which is far faster than a disk and saves SSD writes. It doesn't replace a
disk swap for hibernation, and it uses some CPU. A common size is half of RAM;
give it a higher priority than any disk swap, so it is used first.

Try it on the running system:
  @[modprobe zram]
  @[zramctl --find --size 4G --algorithm zstd]   (prints e.g. /dev/zram0)
  @[mkswap /dev/zram0 && swapon --priority 100 /dev/zram0]
  @[swapon --show]                               (lists all swap)

To set it up at every boot on the installed system, put those lines into
/etc/sysconfig/bootmisc (SMGL runs that file at boot when it is executable:
@[chmod +x /etc/sysconfig/bootmisc]). zram needs no fstab line.


6. COMPILING IN RAM (SORCERY'S TMPFS OPTION)
--------------------------------------------
tmpfs is a filesystem in RAM. With sorcery's TMPFS option on, each spell is
unpacked and compiled in a tmpfs mounted on its build directory under
/usr/src, and only the finished result is installed to disk: faster builds
and far fewer writes to the disk. Big spells mark themselves TMPFS=off and
build on disk anyway (glibc, libreoffice, ...).

The tmpfs can grow to half of RAM; anything beyond spills into swap (slow) or
the build fails with "no space left on device". So it suits machines with
plenty of RAM (16 GiB or more is comfortable); leave it off on small ones, or
add zram/swap. Turn it on (in the installed system) with
  @[sorcery]   then Options -> Feature Menu -> TMPFS
or by adding @[TMPFS=on] to /etc/sorcery/local/config.

7. STARTING OVER ON A USED DISK
-------------------------------
A new partition table only records where partitions start and end; the data
inside stays. A new partition at the same place as an old one therefore still
shows the old filesystem in @[lsblk -f] (type, label, UUID), even after a
reboot. That is harmless: mkfs writes over it (and asks first). To begin from
a truly blank disk anyway, before partitioning (this erases everything on it):
  @[wipefs -a /dev/sdX1 /dev/sdX2 /dev/sdX]  remove the filesystem and
                                             partition table signatures
                                             (partitions first, then the disk)
  @[blkdiscard /dev/nvme0n1]                 SSDs: discard every block; takes
                                             seconds, then the disk reads as
                                             zeros (not for hard disks)
Check the result with @[lsblk -f] (no FSTYPE left).
